              text: 'The Panchatantra is a set of stories written in the Sanskrit language more than two thousand years ago. It was written by a well-known Sanskrit scholar, Vishnu Sharma. He wrote this collection to teach valuable lessons on morals and values to everyone, especially children, in a very friendly way. The word Pancha means five and Tantra means codes or principles. All the stories in the Panchatantra are categorised into five groups. These stories are so popular that they have been read by children across the world for centuries. These stories have been translated into all Indian languages and many foreign languages, such as English, German, Italian, Arabic, Japanese, Spanish, and many others. Here is one such interesting story from the Panchatantra — PANCHATANTRA Visual Arts.indd Visual Arts.indd It is not my fault, sir! A stronger lion stopped me on my way. He said he will eat me before you. One day, it was the hare‛s turn. He walked slowly, trying to delay his fate. Fine. But if one ever fails to come, I will kill you all! We promise to send you one animal every day. Why kill us all when one a day is enough for you? The animals lived in constant fear. One day, they devised a plan and approached the lion with a proposal. In a forest there lived a ferocious lion, Bh a suraka. To show his strength, he would kill more animals than he could eat. You miserable creature , you are late! Because of you, I will kill all the animals tomorrow! Every day, an animal presented itself to the lion for his meal.',
