              text: 'DANCES AROUND ME Every region in our country has a unique culture and tradition. It also has its unique music and dance. Chirmi dance of Rajasthan Chham dance of Sikkim Kommu Koya dance of Andhra Pradesh Sammi dance of Punjab Dance.indd Dance.indd Many of our local dances are connected to nature. For example, you learned a Kannada song in music class, “Aadona banni kanna muchale tugona banni uyyale . ” The song describes trees, birds, and the nature. Or you can choose nature-related songs, like Rabindra Sangeet’s “Phule phule dhole dhole...” describing flowers, rivers, and birds. Put together all the dance steps with gentle and vigorous movements, hand gestures, and facial expressions and dance to the rhythm of the song. My Dance and Nature Note for Teachers: Refer to a nature song from music class and explain its meaning. Refer to the nature songs in the QR Code on the previous page. Activity 18.1 Bansuri • Grade Dance.indd Dance.indd Find out about your regional dance style, traditional music, and other art forms. What kind of music accompanies your dance? Do you find paintings and images of local dance in your region? Now, choose one of the dances of your region and dance. Learning My Regional Dance Note for Teachers: Taking the students to a performance of a traditional dance form will help, them in understanding the different elements of the particular dance. This can be discussed in class to let the children share their thoughts and feelings about the dance performance.',
